.TH EUCLID-CLI-EQS-DELETE-MESSAGE 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eqs-delete-message \- delete a single message
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli eqs delete-message
.BR \-\-receipt\-handle | \-r
.I HANDLE
.br
.B euclid-cli eqs delete-message
.BR \-\-message\-id | \-m
.I MESSAGEID
.SH DESCRIPTION
Deletes one message. Exactly one of the two ways of naming it is needed;
giving neither is refused.
.SH THE TWO WAYS TO NAME IT
.TP
.B \-\-receipt\-handle
The standard way, and the one a consumer uses: a handle is what
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-receive-messages (1)
hands back, and deleting by it is acknowledging work that was received.
This is the AWS SQS-compatible path.
.TP
.B \-\-message\-id
Deletes the message directly, whether or not it has ever been received \(em
including one that is
.I AVAILABLE
or
.IR DELAYED ,
sitting in the queue waiting for a consumer. This bypasses the
receipt-handle lease entirely and has no AWS SQS equivalent. It is for an
operator removing a message that is poisoning a queue, not for a
consumer.
.PP
If both are given the receipt handle is used and the message id is
ignored.
.SH IT DOES NOT SAY WHETHER ANYTHING WAS DELETED
Deleting a message that is not there succeeds. The delete is a filtered
removal, and a filter that matches nothing removes nothing and reports no
error \(em so
.B 0
means "there is now no such message", not "there was one and it is gone".
.PP
This matters most for a receipt handle that has expired. When a message's
visibility timeout runs out it becomes
.I AVAILABLE
again and its receipt handle is cleared, so the handle a consumer is
holding no longer matches anything. Deleting by it then removes nothing
and still exits
.BR 0 ,
while the message is redelivered to somebody else. A consumer that took
too long does not find out here; it finds out because the work comes round
again.
.PP
So a script cannot use the exit status to learn whether a message existed.
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-get-message (1)
answers that, and
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-get-message-count (1)
answers it in aggregate.
.SH OUTPUT
Nothing on success. A command that printed an acknowledgement would be
claiming something it does not know \(em see above.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-receipt\-handle " " \fIHANDLE\fR
Receipt handle of a received message, as returned by
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-receive-messages (1).
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-message\-id " " \fIMESSAGEID\fR
Message id. Deletes the message even if it has never been received.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
There is now no message with that handle or id. It may or may not have
been there to begin with.
.TP
.B 1
Neither
.B \-\-receipt\-handle
nor
.B \-\-message\-id
was given, the caller may not delete from the queue, or the server could
not be reached.
.SH EXAMPLES
Acknowledge received work:
.RS
.nf
euclid\-cli eqs delete\-message \-\-receipt\-handle "$HANDLE"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Remove a message nobody has received, by id:
.RS
.nf
euclid\-cli eqs delete\-message \-\-message\-id 66f1c2a9e4b0
.fi
.RE
.PP
Confirm it is gone, which the delete itself does not tell you:
.RS
.nf
euclid\-cli eqs delete\-message \-\-message\-id 66f1c2a9e4b0
if euclid\-cli eqs get\-message \-\-message\-id 66f1c2a9e4b0 \e
        > /dev/null 2>&1; then
    echo "still there" >&2
fi
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eqs (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-receive-messages (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-get-message (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-set-message-visibility (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-purge-queue (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eqs-get-message-count (1)
.SH AUTHOR
